The Macedonians held a referendum on whether to change the country's name.
Macedonians held a general election on Sunday on whether to change Macedonia’s name to “Republic of North Macedonia,” a move that would resolve a decades-long dispute with the European Union and Greece over their efforts to join NATO.
Greece, which used to be called Macedonia, has maintained the attitude that the name of its northern neighbours claims ownership of Greek territory, and opposes joining NATO and the EU.
The two governments saw a compromise based on the proposed new name in June, but nationalist opponents argue that the change of name would undermine the ethnic characteristics that Macedonia's Slavs are the main population.
President Gjorge Ivanov said he would not vote in the national general election, and the anti-vote movement questioned whether the turnout could meet at least 50 percent of the national elections required to be validated.
The questions on the ballot in the national general election include: “Are you in favor of your approval of your agreement with Greece and your membership in NATO and the EU.”
Proponents of the renaming, including Zoran Zaev, argue that it is a price worth paying to seek the accession of institutions such as the EU and NATO for Macedonia, one of the countries emerging from the collapse of Yugoslavia.
"I'm here today for the future of my country, I'm here to vote for the young people of Macedonia, so young people will be able to live freely under the umbrella of the European Union, because it means a safer life for all of us," said Olivera Georgijevska, 79-year-old in Skopje.
Although it was not legally tied up, he said that a sufficient number of members of the National Assembly would comply with the results of the vote in order to solidify the results of the vote.
The name change requires more than two-thirds approval of the National Assembly.
The Central Election Commission said there were no reports of negativity until 1pm.
However, voter turnout stopped at just 16 percent, compared to the previous congressional elections in 2016, when 66 percent of registered voters cast their ballots.
"I'm here to vote for my children, for our position in Europe," said Gjose Tanevski, a voter in Skopje.
In Strumica on September 30, 2018, Macedonia's Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, along with his wife Zorica and his son Dushko, voted to vote in a referendum to change the name of Macedonia that will pave the way for NATO and the European Union.
In front of a parliament in Strumicha, Vladimir Kavardarkov, fifty-four, raised the chair in front of the tent, set up by those opposed to the general election, and was preparing for a small performance.
"We are in favor of NATO and the EU, but we want to join proudly, not through the back door," Kavardakov said.
"We're a poor country, but we've got dignity.
“If you don’t want to accept us from Macedonia, you can turn to the other side, such as China and Russia, and become part of the European-Asian unification.”
Prime Minister Joyeb said NATO accession would bring more support to Macedonia, which has an unemployment rate of more than 20 per cent.
"I believe that a large number of people will be favorable, because more than 80 percent of our people are favorable to the EU and NATO," he said after Zoev threw his vote.
He said the “pro-choice” outcome would be a “confirmation of our future.”
According to a poll released on Monday by Macedonia's agency for polls, 30 to 43 percent of voters said they would take part in the referendum, falling short of the required turnout.
Other polls conducted on Telma TV in Macedonia found that fifty-seven percent of respondents planned to vote on Sunday.
