Every time I spoke publicly, no matter what I'm talking about, it's full of accusations on social media about centrism, that I shouldn't be in the Labour Party, that I'm not going to be in the Labour Party, that I'm going to drop my next job.
And that’s not my experience.
In fact, I'm more fortunate than a few of my colleagues, because my comments toward me tended to be political.
I admire the professionalism and determination of my colleagues, who face sexual or racial discrimination every day, but never avoid it.
One of the most frustrating aspects of the current regime is the way in which various insults are normalized.
Jeremy Corbyn insisted last week that Labour should foster a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we are no longer a great political party, and all the “unconfident” agreements or changes in selection make political parties even more narrow.
I’ve heard a lot of advice over the past year that if you don’t avoid attention-grabbing behavior and don’t assert yourself too strongly, it’s going to be “okay.”
But that wasn’t what I was trying to do in politics.
Thirty-twenty-two years ago, when I joined the Labour Party as a student, angry that Thatcher's indifference literally allowed the comprehensive school classroom to collapse, I've been seeking to fight for better public services for the sake of the people who need it.
I've never hidden my political views, including in the last election.
No one in the House of Representatives has ever been confused about my policy position and the field of conflict with the current leadership.
All I have to say to those of us who started Friday’s agreement is that when the country is heading for a Brexit that harms homes, industries and public services, I don’t understand the desire to waste time and energy trying to show loyalty to a Labour leader.
But really, the only message I'm going to convey isn't for the House of Representatives momentum, it's for my constituencies, whether it's Labour or not, and I'm proud to be able to contribute to you, and we won't be able to prevent you from doing what we believe is best for all of you.
Chris Leslie is a Member of Parliament for Nottingham East.
