Across Bolivia, surrounded by land, the memory of losing its coastline in a resource conflict with Chile in the 19th century is still clear, and that's why we're yearning for one more voyage to the Pacific.
That hope has probably peaked in decades, as Bolivia, after five years of deliberations, is awaiting a ruling from the International Court of Justice on October 1st.
"Bolivia has acceleration, unity and composure, and, of course, we have positive expectations of the results," said Bolivian diplomat Roberto Calzadilla.
Across the country, many Bolivians will look at the International Court of Justice's ruling on the big screen, hoping that Chile will be obliged to negotiate an independent exit from the sea after a decades-long short-sprinching meeting in Hague.
