John Edward: Linguistic technology is essential as a global citizen
Scotland’s private schools maintain a track record of academic excellence, and performance retention leads to outstanding test results in 2018, which is a further improvement in individual and overall success in sports, arts, music, and other areas of hard work.
With as many as 30,000 students from across Scotland, represented by the Scottish Private Schools Council (SCIS), these schools strive to provide the best service to their students and parents.
Private schools aim to prepare students for adult education and higher education, careers of their choice, and the status of their students as global citizens.
As an educational institution capable of planning and implementing a personalized school curriculum, we continue to witness that modern language continues to be a popular and desired elective among schools.
Nelson Mandela said, “If you talk in the language he recognizes, it’s going to get into his head.
If you speak it in his language, it's going to be engraved on his heart."
This powerfully tells us that when we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries, we can’t rely solely on English.
Looking at this year's recent test results, you'll see that the language ranks first in the league table with the highest acceptance rate within independent schools.
68% of students who studied a foreign language all had high grade A credits.
According to data collected from 74 member schools at SCIS, 72% of students received A credits in Mandarin, 72% of students who studied German, 69% of students who studied French, and 63% of students who studied Spanish also received A credits.
This shows that independent schools in Scotland are supporting foreign languages as an essential skill in the future for children and young people.
At present, the language is treated as a choice of subjects, such as STEM subjects (science, technology, engineering, and mathematics), such as independent school curriculum.
According to a survey conducted by the UK Commission for Employment and Skills in 2014, 17 per cent are due to a lack of language skills as employers have struggled to fill the vacancies.
As a result, language skills are becoming more and more essential for young people to prepare for future careers.
As there are more career opportunities in the future where language is needed, these capabilities are essential in a globalized world.
No matter which career you choose, those who have learned a second language will have the practical benefits of having this lifelong ability in the future.
By being able to communicate directly with people from abroad, people who are automatically multilingual are ahead of the competition.
According to YouGov, a 2013 poll of more than 4,000 British adults found that 75 per cent don't speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation, and that French is the only language spoken at a double-digit rate of 15 percent.
That's why it's so important for children today to invest in language education.
Using multiple languages, especially in developing countries, will give children a better opportunity to get a meaningful job.
Within Scotland, the languages taught by each school will be diverse.
Many schools will focus on more classic modern languages, while others will teach the most important languages in the UK, such as Mandarin and Japanese.
No matter what kind of interest your child is, there will always be plenty of languages to choose from within an independent school, along with faculty who are experts in this field.
Scotland’s independent schools are committed to providing a learning environment to prepare their children for anything in the future and provide them with the skills needed to succeed.
In a global business environment, there is no denying that language continues to be very important for the country's future, so this must be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages really should be seen as “international communication skills.”
Independent schools will continue to provide these choices, diversities, and excellence to young people in Scotland.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is a member of the Scottish Independent School Board.
