North one-to-one disarmament without trust is “impossibly possible.”
North Korea’s foreign minister told the United Nations on Saturday that ongoing sanctions are deepening distrust of the U.S. and there is no way for North Korea to unilaterally give up its nuclear weapons under those circumstances.
At the World Organisation's annual general meeting, Ri Yongho said North Korea has taken "significant friendly measures," suspending nuclear and missile tests over the past year, dismantling nuclear test sites, and vowing not to spread nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
“But I don’t see a corresponding response from the United States,” he said.
“Without trust in the United States, there would be no confidence in our national security, and in such a situation, there would be no unilateral disarmament.”
Foreign Minister Lee reiterated North Korea’s dissatisfactions familiar with U.S. resistance to “step-by-step” denuclearization, which will be rewarded by North Korea’s gradual actions, but his comments were significant in that he did not reject unilateral denuclearization as North Korea did in the past.
At the first summit between the U.S. president and the North Korean leader held in Singapore on the 12th of last month, Foreign Minister Lee said that Kim Jong-un pledged his commitment to "denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula," referring to a joint statement that President Trump had pledged to ensure North Korea's security.
North Korea has sought the official end of the 1950–53 Korean War, but the U.S. says North Korea must give up its nuclear weapons first.
The U.S. has also rejected calls for tougher international sanctions against North Korea.
"The U.S. insists on denuclearization first, increases the level of pressure with sanctions in order to achieve its purpose in a coercive way, and opposes the declaration of war," he said.
"The perception that sanctions can bring us to our knees is a dream of people who are ignorant of us.
The problem, however, is that with continued sanctions, our distrust is deepening.”
Foreign Minister Lee did not comment on President Trump's plans for a second summit, which he highlighted at the United Nations earlier this week.
Instead, he emphasized three meetings over the past five months between Kim and South Korean President Moon Jae-in, adding: "If the parties to this denuclearization issue were not the United States or South Korea, the denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula wouldn't be in such a stalemate."
Even so, Lee's tone of speech was markedly different from what he said at the U.N. General Assembly last year that it was unavoidable to target the U.S. mainland with North Korean rockets after Trump defined Kim as a "rocket man" on his suicide mission.
Trump, who threatened to "totally destroy" North Korea last year, said at the U.N. this year that Mr. Kim was courageous to take measures to disarmament, but that he still had to do a lot of work and maintain sanctions until North Korea denuclearized.
"It doesn't matter if it's two years, three years or five months," Trump said on Wednesday.
China and Russia insist they should reward Pyongyang for the measures taken by the U.N. Security Council.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Wednesday: “We must continue to vigorously enforce the Security Council’s sanctions until we realize a full and final proven denuclearization.”
The Security Council has unanimously tightened sanctions on North Korea since 2006 to halt funding for Pyongyang's nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Separately from the U.N. General Assembly, Pompeo said he would visit Pyongyang next month to prepare for a second summit.
Mr. Pompeo has visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip wasn’t smooth.
He said there had been progress in July and had left Pyongyang, but within a few hours, North Korea had denounced him as a "thug-like demand."
North Korea, during a meeting with Moon this month, promised to dismantle missile sites and nuclear complexes if the U.S. takes "responsive measures."
He says Kim has conveyed "corporate measures," including the security measures promised by President Trump in Singapore and moves to normalize relations with the United States.
Harvard students take classes to get plenty of rest
This year's new course at Harvard University has allowed all undergraduates to sleep better in order to fight the growing macho culture through caffeine-addicted 'All Night'.
One scholar found that students at the world’s top universities are often ignorant of what’s very basic about how to take care of themselves.
Charles Czeisler, a professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School, Brigham, designed this course, and he believes it's the first of its kind in the United States.
He was inspired to start the course after giving talks about the effects of sleep deprivation on learning.
At the end of the class, a girl came up to me and said, "Why do you listen to these teachings when you're older?"
She said that no one had ever told her about the importance of sleep, and it surprised me,' he said in an interview with The Telegraph.
For the first time this year, the course explains to students how good sleeping habits can help them improve their academic and athletic performance, and how important it is to promote overall happiness.
Paul Barreira, a professor of mental health at Harvard Medical School, said the university decided to introduce this process when it discovered that students experienced severe sleep deprivation during the week.
The one-hour course involves a series of interactive tasks.
There's an image of a dormitory in one section, where students can click on coffee cups, curtains, trainers and books to hear about the effects of caffeine and light and how athletic activity is affected by lack of sleep, and the importance of bedtime routines.
In another chapter, participants were told how prolonged sleep deprivations could increase the risk of heart attacks, strokes, depression, and cancer.
Maps with campus maps and interactive icons make participants think about their daily lives.
“We know it won’t change the behavior of our students immediately.
But we believe they have the right to know, just as you have the right to know the health effects of smoking," Professor Cheisler added.
He added that there is still a culture of self-esteem about 'shripping the night', adding that modern technology and the ever-increasing pressure on students means that sleep deprivation is an increasing problem.
Sleep deprivations make the brain into a mode of starvation, he says, so in order not to gain weight, it has to be a "secret weapon" for students to combat stress, fatigue, and anxiety, he said.
Nineteen-year-old Raymond So, who studies chemical and physical biology in California, helped design this course as Professor Cheisler took one of his lectures during his first year at Harvard University last year.
He said the course opened his eyes and inspired him to push the courses around the campus.
The next step is to require all graduate students to complete a similar study program before entering a competing institution.
Professor Kaiser advised that students should take into account when they will go to bed and when it will take place, and should keep in mind the detrimental effects of 'blue light' emitted by electronic screens and LED lights, which can lead to sleep problems by lifting the rhythms of your cycle out of normal conditions.
