Year-end: Inventor of the Geiger counter
On one page of the "Sunday Morning" yearbook: September 30, 1882, 136 years ago today, and... the day that future physicist Johannes "Hans" Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger eventually developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactive materials, an invention that led to a device known as the Geiger counter.
Since then, the Geiger counter, which has been at the center of science, has also become the centerpiece of popular culture in the 1950 film "Bell of the Coronado," in which Roy Rogers and Dale Evans appear as cowboy scientists who are nowhere to be found:
Man: "What the hell is that?"
Rogers: "It's a Geiger counter used to determine the location of radioactive minerals such as uranium.
If you put on these earphones, you’ll actually be able to hear the effects of the atoms emitted by mineral radioactivity.”
Evans: "Hey, it's definitely exploding right now!"
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just a few days after his 63rd birthday.
However, the invention with his name continues to exist.
A new cancer vaccine can teach your immune system to “see” malignant cells.
A new cancer vaccine could teach the immune system to 'report' and kill malignant cells.
Vaccines teach the immune system to be recognized as targets for treating malignant cells.
The method involves extracting immune cells from patients and changing them in the laboratory.
