Companies call this "personal empowerment through data."
According to the company’s CEO, John Bruce, the company’s idea for Inrupt is to provide resources, processes, and appropriate technology to help everyone use Solid products.
The company is now comprised of a security platform acquired by Berners-Lee, Bruce and IBM, some on-site developers who have contracted to work on this project, and a community of volunteer coders.
Starting this week, tech developers around the world will be able to create their own decentralized apps using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Bersley said he and his team aren’t talking to Facebook and Google about whether all of their business models will introduce a complete change that will be completely upgraded overnight.
"We're not asking for their permission."
Insrupt’s “The mission is to provide an ecosystem that helps protect the integrity and quality of the new web built on commercial energy and solids,” Berners-Lee wrote in a Saturday edition of Medium.
In 1994, Berners Lee transformed the Internet by establishing the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Bernsley has voiced influence in the net neutrality debate.
Even during the launch of Inrupt, Berners Lee will remain the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation, and the Open Data Research Institute.
“I’m incredibly optimistic about the next era on the web,” Bernersley added.
Bernard Vann: World Celebrity Commemoration of the Victorian Cross of the First World War
The only British clerk who triumphed as a combatant in the Victorian crusaders during the First World War has been commemorated for more than a hundred years in his homeland.
The lieutenant-in-leavy Rev. Bernard Van received the award by attacking Bellenglise and Lehaucourt on September 29, 1918.
Four days later, however, he was killed by a sniper and had no idea that he had received the highest British medal.
At a parade held in Rushden (Northamptonshire) on Saturday, two of his grandchildren unveiled the monument.
Michael Vann, one of his grandchildren, said it was a "sacrified symbol" that the monument revealed about his grandfather's award-winning career exactly a hundred years later.
According to the London Gazette, on September 29, 1918, Lieutenant Van led his battalion across the Canal de Saint-Quentin, "roughing through a very thick fog and under the concentrationation of artillery and machine guns."
He later hurried up to the firing line, leading the line forward as the "greatest warrior," and then rushing to the field gun with a single body, knocking down three detachments.
Lieutenant Van was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918, just a month before the end of the war.
Seventy-two-year-old Michael Van said his grandfather's behaviour was, "I know it's something I'll never be able to keep up with, but it's unpretentious."
He and his older brother, Dr. James Vann, also laid a wreath after a parade conducted by the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
"It's a great honor to be involved in the parade procession," he said, adding that "the courage of a true hero is being demonstrated by the support it receives from many people."
