He said: "To this day it was the real things that gave me the strength and the courage to feel less and felt differently."
He said he wanted to prove that he was “something,” and that he didn’t let everyone down when he eventually found out about his sanity.
The two-time bronze medalist became a well-respected LGBT activist and took advantage of his appearance at this year’s Commonwealth Games in Australia to make sure more countries don’t punish homosexuals.
He was lucky to be able to live openly without waves, and because he wanted to give “hope” to others.
The winners of the three world championships - Dustin Lance Black, an American filmmaker whom he met in 2013 - fell in love with a man and said, "I was surprised."
