Germany's new warship postponed again
The German Navy’s newest frigate should have been commissioned in 2014 to replace an aging Cold War-era warship, but due to its faulty system and snowballing costs, it will not be able to get there until at least next year, local media reported.
According to the Die Zeit newspaper, which quoted a military spokesperson, the approval of “Rheinland-Pfalz,” the delivery ship of the Baden-Uertemberg-class frigate, was postponed to the first half of 2019.
The ship should have joined the Navy in 2014, but after a distressed delivery, the problem plagued the fate of this ambitious project.
The four Baden-Urttemberg-class ships ordered by the Navy will be a replacement for the old Bremen-class frigate.
It's believed to be equipped with some stealth technologies, such as powerful artillery, anti-aircraft and anti-aircraft missiles, as well as radar reduction, infrared, and acoustic signatures.
Other important features include a longer maintenance period, which means you should be able to place the latest frigates on your home port for up to two years.
However, the ongoing delay means that state-of-the-art warships, which allow Germany to project power overseas, will already be obsolete by the time they get into service, Die Zeit said.
The unlucky F125 frigate went viral last year when the German Navy officially refused to take the vessel and returned it to the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
This was the first time the Navy had completed its delivery and returned the vessel to the shipyard.
Little is known about the background of his return, but German media have cited a number of decisive “software and hardware defects” that rendered warships obsolete when deployed on combat missions.
Software glitches were particularly important, as the Baden-Urttemberg-class vessel would be operated by about one-and-a-half sailors, only half of the workforce for the old Bremen class.
In addition, it has been found that because this ship is dramatically overweight, its performance is degraded, and the Navy's ability to add future upgrades is limited.
“Rheinland-Pfalz” is believed to be twice as heavy as a similar-grade ship used by the Germans during World War II.
In addition to hardware defects, the price tag for the entire project, including the training of the crew, is also problematic.
That's more than €2.2 billion (£36bn).
In light of recent warnings that Germany's naval forces are diminishing, the issues that captivate the most recent frigates become particularly important.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, Germany's head of parliament's defense committee, acknowledged that the navy was actually "depletining ships that could be placed."
The official said the problem was snowballing over time, as the old vessels were dismantled but alternative vessels were not provided.
He lamented that there was not a single ship capable of joining the navy among the Baden-Uertemberg-class frigates.
The National Trust intercepts the secret creatures of bats.
A new study taking place on a private site in the Scottish Highlands is aimed at uncovering how bats use this landscape to hunt their prey.
We hope that this discovery will renew the behavior of unique flying mammals and help guide the conservation activities of the future.
Research by scientists at the National Trust for Scotland will follow brown long-eared bats and Daubento bats, as well as common soprano piffistrels and soprano piffistrels, at the Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special recording devices will be placed in key locations around the property to keep track of bat activity throughout the season.
NHS staff and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using portable detectors.
The expert sound analysis of every recording will determine the frequency of bats and what species are doing.
Habitat maps and reports will be generated to create detailed landscape paintings of their behavior.
Rob Dewar, NTS’s nature conservation adviser, hopes that the results will reveal what habitat areas are most important to bats and how they are used by their respective species.
This information will help determine the benefits of habitat management operations, such as pastures, as well as how best to maintain woodlands for bats and other related species.
The number of bat populations across Scotland and the United Kingdom has declined considerably over the past century.
They are under threat of construction and development work, which affects the loss of nests and habitats.
Wind turbines and lighting can also be dangerous, as this can cause not only chemical processing of raw paper and building materials, but also attacks on pets.
Bats aren't really blind.
However, due to nocturnal hunting habits, their ears are more useful when it comes to catching prey than the eyes.
They use sophisticated eco-location techniques to pinpoint bugs and obstacles in flight paths.
The NTS, which manages more than two hundred and seventy-six historical buildings, thirty-eight important gardens and seventy-six thousand hectares of land, is taking bats very seriously.
Ten experienced professionals conduct regular investigations and inspections, and sometimes rescue operations.
For the first time in Scotland, the organization has designated a special bat sanctuary in Thresive, located on the Dumfries and Galloway. Eight of Scotland's ten species of bats currently live in these special protected areas.
According to David Thompson, it’s an ideal habitat for bats.
In addition, David said, "There is a very suitable area for bats in the thrift."
"There's an old building, there's a lot of hardwood trees, and there's everything you need to inhabit bats.
However, there is still so much we don’t know about bats, and you’ll come to understand the many things you’ll need to thrive while protecting bats in this territory and elsewhere.”
David stressed that it is important to check the essential requirements before administering it to bat habitats, as a result there could be as many as four hundred female and young bats dying if unintentionally destroyed, and as a result, bats in the area could be wiped out.
At present, bats are protected and are legally prohibited from destroying breeding grounds, including killing, harassing, or interfering with bats.
Elizabeth Ferrell, who works for the Scottish Bat Protection Agency, asked him to actively participate in bat protection efforts.
"You still have to figure out a lot of things about bats and you don't know how the different species that live in Scotland thrive," Ferrell said.
