Brexit: UK 'would be left behind and regretd' over carmaker's pull-out
British Industry Minister Greg Clark noted that Britain’s industry status, which has held a leading position in automotive manufacturing until now, will plummet and that it will be “regretful.”
He also told the BBC that Toyota will temporarily suspend production at its Bernaston plant near the derby if the UK leaves the EU without an agreement.
"We have to come to an agreement," Secretary Clark stressed.
According to Japanese automakers, if Brexit is carried out without an agreement, there will be delays in crossing the border and the need to keep up with job cuts.
The Burnaston plant, which manufactures the Toyota Oris and Avengers, has exported 90 percent of the 150,000 cars produced last year to members of the European Union.
"From my point of view, if the U.K. gets Brexit at the end of March, we will have no choice but to stop production," Marvin Cook, Burnaston’s head of management for Toyota, noted.
Other British automakers, such as Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover, have also expressed anxiety about leaving the EU without an agreement on how cross-border trade will take place.
BMW officials, for example, have said they are set to close a mini-factory in Oxford starting next month, when Brexit takes place.
If you look at the automaker’s announcements, they’re very worried that there will be huge disruptions to the supply chain if Brexit goes through without an agreement.
The Toyota production line takes the “JIT” way, that is, the “JIT” method of building the vehicle, not the method of ordering at that time, so the parts needed to produce the ordered car arrive every thirty-seven minutes from a supplier from a British and EU member state.
If the U.K. does not come up with a deal on March 29th, Brexit will lead to problems with parts crossing the border, which will immediately lead to delays and shortages in the supply of parts.
Since it is not possible to keep more than a day's worth of stock at the Toyota factory in Derbyshire, production will be disrupted, plant officials said.
"We need to plan specifically not to go through an unnecessary verification process on the border," Clark said, when Prime Minister Theresa May plans future relations with the EU.
"We desperately need a deal, we need to come up with the best possible agreement not only if we want to seize the chances of Brexit, as well as the success we see right in front of our eyes," Clarke said on BBC Radio 4's programme today.
"Considering the concerns of Toyota, as well as other automakers, we need to be able to make sure that the supply chain configurations that have been successfully operated so far will be ensured in the future."
It's hard to predict how long Toyota's going to stop production, warning that in the long run, production competitiveness will be reduced due to additional costs, and jobs will be reduced.
Peter Sovalis, the union’s vice-president for twenty-four years working at the Burnastone plant, said union members were worried, adding: “In my experience, jobs that once disappeared have never been restored.”
A government spokesperson said: "We will implement an accurate and credible plan to ensure that relations with the EU are well-established in the future."
According to the White House's announcement, President Trump will again postpone a meeting with Secretary Rosenstein.
As hearings of Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanano continue, the meeting between President Donald Trump and Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein, a sensitive issue, will be postponed by "one more week," the White House spokesman said on Sunday.
Rosenstein is overseeing the work of special counsel Robert Mueller, who is conducting an investigative investigation into Russia's interference in the presidential election, Trump's support and Russia's relationship, as well as the possibility that the president could interfere with the implementation of justice in the future.
