Jack Ross: 'The ultimate goal is to coach Scotland'
Sunderland manager Jack Ross said the "final goal" he pursued was to become a Scottish manager at the right time.
Forty-two-year-old Ross, from Scotland, enjoys the joy of getting promoted to a North-East club, who is currently in third place in the first division with a three-goal lead.
He moved to the Stadium of Lights this summer after returning St Mirren to the Scottish Premiership last season.
In an interview with the BBC's Scottish Sports Sound, Ross said: "As an athlete, I wanted to play for my country.
But I was a second-class player, and I couldn’t climb anymore.”
"Since I was a kid, I grew up watching the Scottish team's game in Hampdon often, and there's always been something that attracted me.
Running a successful club is the only way to seize the opportunity.”
Prior to Ross, Dick Advocat, David Moyes, Sam Allerdice, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyette, and Paulo di Canio coached Sunderland.
Aloa Athletic's full-time manager said he could follow Ross, who successfully led a club that had been relegated from Bansley and Ipswich Town, without fear.
"At the moment, it's my goal to get this club back into the Premier League.
Given the structure and facilities of this club, you have to belong to the Premier League."
“It’s not an easy task to get back into the Premier League, but it’s probably just for me to say it’s a success that brought the club back.”
Ross has only served as an assistant manager at Doombarton and only for three years after working fifteen months as Hart's coaching staff.
Later, Aloa supported him from being relegated to the third team, and he led St. Mirren to a championship victory next season on the brink of relegation.
After his career at Clyde, Hartlepool, Poker, St Mirren and Hamilton, Ross said he is more comfortable now than ever.
Reminiscing about taking on Aloa, he said, "That would have been the real turning point."
"Seriously, I believed the managerial position was better than playing the game directly.
The game was pretty good, I had a pretty good life, and it might seem strange for me to leave my job because I had the right honor.
But it was so hard to play the game.
I had to do a lot of work every week.
Every time I came into the game, I felt intense stress and pressure, so I felt that the management would be more suitable for me.
I've always wanted to work as a manager, but now I'm doing it, and now I feel most comfortable doing the best job I've ever done since I became an adult."
The full interview with Sports Sound on Friday, September 30th, will be available on Radio Scotland from 12:00 - 13:00 (UK Summer Time).
