2004: On December 26, a menacing quake on the west coast of Sumatra's northern province of Ache caused a tsunami that struck 14 countries and killed 226,000 people along the coast of the Indian Ocean, more than half of which came from the province of Ache.
In 2005, a strong earthquake hit the west coast of Sumatra in succession at the end of March and the beginning of April.
Hundreds have been killed on the island of Nias on the coast of Sumatra.
In 2006, a magnitude 6.8 earthquake struck the most populous island of Java in Indonesia, causing a tsunami hitting the south coast, killing close to 700 people.
2009: An earthquake of magnitude 7.6 occurred in Padang, the capital of the western province of Sumatra.
More than 1,100 people died.
In 2010, an earthquake of magnitude 7.5 struck one of Sumatra's Mentaway Islands, triggering a 10m tsunami, destroying dozens of villages and killing about 300 people.
2016: A weak quake hits the regency of Fidijaya in the province of Ache, causing disturbances for those who were reminded of the heinous earthquake and tsunami.
There was no tsunami, but more than a hundred people were killed because of the collapsed building.
In 2018, a menacing earthquake struck the island of Lombok, a tourist attraction in Indonesia, killing more than five hundred people; most of the damage occurred in the north of the island.
