At the time, Patricia Schröder, a member of the Colorado House of Representatives, worked with Gary Hart to run for the presidency.
In 1987, Mr. Hart gave up the race because he was embroiled in an affair on a ship called Monkey Business. At that time, Senator Schröder was deeply frustrated. She felt that there was no reason not to consider herself running for president.
"It wasn't a decision I made after I thought about it closely," she said, laughing thirty years later.
“There were already seven more candidates in the race, and what they didn’t need the most was another.
Someone called it “Snow White and the Seven Dwarves.”
Since she started her campaign late, she was pushed out of the fundraising campaign, so she vowed not to participate in the race if she didn't raise two million dollars.
It was a struggle.
She realized that some of her supporters, who would give $1,000 to male contestants, would give her only $250.
“Do they think I’ll get a small amount?” she wondered.
When she announced that she wouldn't campaign, she was so overwhelmed by feelings such as an appreciation of the people who supported her, a system that made it too difficult to raise money, a setback to a system that was difficult to target voters rather than delegates, and anger over sexism.
“You probably thought I had a nervous breakdown,” Schröder said, recalling how the media reacted to her actions.
“I thought Klinex was my corporate sponsor.
I remember what I thought they'd write on my gravestone.
“She was crying.”
How the U.S.-China trade war will help Beijing
The beginning of the trade war between the U.S. and China was likely to erupt, and while the battle is still far from over, the rift between the two countries may be beneficial to China in the long run, experts say.
U.S. President Donald Trump issued his first warning earlier this year, charging taxes on major Chinese exports such as solar panels, steel and aluminum.
This week's most important point was the application of new tariffs on $200bn worth of items (£150bn), which would tax half of all goods coming into the United States from China.
China retaliated in the same way each time, most recently imposing tariffs ranging from 5 percent to 10 percent on $60 billion worth of U.S. goods.
China has vowed to retaliate against the United States, and the world’s second-largest economy is not likely to make an eye contact anytime soon.
It would mean accommodating U.S. demands to soften Washington, but it would be too embarrassing for Chinese President Xi Jinping to keep his head down to the United States in public.
But experts say that if China can make good use of its options, U.S. trade war pressures will reduce the interdependence of both economies, which will work in China’s favor in the long run.
“The fact that rapid political decisions in Washington or Beijing could create conditions that could trigger an economic crisis in any one country is actually much more dangerous than observers have previously acknowledged,” said Abigail Grace, Asia researcher at the New American Security Center.
The foreign minister says Syria is 'ready' to return refugees
Syria says it is ready for the voluntary return of refugees and is appealing for help to rebuild the country, devastated by more than seven years of long wars.
Foreign Minister Walid al-Mualem said the situation in Syria is improving at the United Nations General Assembly.
He said: "Today's progress in the fight against terrorism makes the situation on the ground more stable and safer."
The government continues to move residents to restore areas destroyed by terrorists to normalcy.
All the conditions for the voluntary return of the refugees are now in place, and they had to leave Syria because of terrorism and unilateral economic measures threatening their daily lives and livelihoods.
The United Nations estimates that more than five and a half million Syrians have fled abroad since the war began in 2011.
Another six million people, who still live in Syria, also need humanitarian assistance.
Al-Mualem said the Syrian government would welcome helping to rebuild this devastated country.
However, he stressed that he would not seek conditional support or assistance from countries that sponsored the civil war.
Europe won the Ryder Cup in Paris.
The European team defeated the U.S. by 16.5 to 10.5 in the 2018 Ryder Cup final at Le Golf National outside Paris, France.
The U.S. has now lost six in a row at the Ryder Cup in Europe and has failed to win in Europe since 1993.
As Denmark-born captain Thomas Bjorn's team scored the 14.5 points needed to beat the U.S., Europe regained its crown.
American star Phil Mickelson, who had struggled for most of the tournament, threw a tee shot into the water on the 16th hole, giving the victory to Francesco Molinari.
Italian golfer Molinari became one of the four who won 5-0–0 after the 1979 tournament with current tournament rules applied and shined in all rounds.
U.S.-based U.S. Jordan Spieth defeated Denmark's Tovyon Olesen 5&4 at the bottom of the European team.
Dustin Johnson, number one in the world rankings, lost two and one to Britain's Ian Polter, whose game may have been his last Ryder Cup.
Spain's Sergio García, a veteran who competed in eight Ryder Cup matches, scored 25.5 career points, making him the biggest winner of all-time as a European player.
“I don’t usually cry today.
It's been a tough year.
I'm so grateful that Thomas picked me up and believed in me.
I'm so happy, I'm very happy I've regained the Cup.
“It’s work for the team and I’m happy to be able to help,” said Garcia, who was thrilled to win the European team.”
He passes the torch to Spanish golfer John Lam, who took down America's legendary golf hero Tiger Woods 2&1 during Sunday's singles match.
"I'm so proud that I can't say I beat Tiger Woods. I grew up watching Woods play," 23-year-old Ram said.
Woods has been defeated in four Ryder Cup matches in France and currently has a 13-21–3 record.
With the exception of Jack Nicklaus, he has fourteen major titles, the second-highest, but it's a strange statistic written by one of the greatest players in history.
The U.S. team struggled to send the ball on the fairway throughout the weekend, with the exception of Patrick Reed, Justin Thomas and Tony Pinau, who played highly competitive golf throughout the tournament.
U.S. captain Jim Furyk said, “I’m proud of these guys, they fought.”
There was a time this morning when we put some pressure on Europe.
We have been defeated.
Congratulations to Thomas.
He’s a great team leader.
All twelve of his players played very well.
We're going to rearrange the team, and we're going to work with the U.S. PGA and the Ryder Cup Committee to move forward.
I am proud to love these twelve men and to be a leader.
You have to pay homage.
“The team played better.”
Reduced concentrations in Red Pinera, Manate and Sarasota
A recent report by the Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission shows that red tide concentrations in some parts of Tampa Bay are generally declining.
According to the Commission, red tides are being observed in parts of Pinera, Manate, Sarasota, Charlotte, and Collier counties, indicating that concentrations are decreasing.
Red tide stretches across about 130miles of coastline from Northern Pineras to Southern Lee County.
The red tide fragments are found about ten miles from Hillsborough County, but are found in fewer areas compared to last week.
Red tides were also observed in Pasco County.
Last week, moderate concentrations were reported in Pinras County, low or high concentrations in Hillsborough County, close to normal or high concentrations in Manate County, as well as normal or high concentrations in Sarasota County and coastal areas, and close or intermediate concentrations in Charlotte County.
Respiratory diseases continue to be reported in Pinera, Manate, Sarasota, Lee and Collier counties.
There were no reports of respiratory illnesses in northwest Florida last week.
